[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1981","stub":{"id":"1981","section":"2. Elizabeth Furnace to Waterlick (Route 55)","altname":"","river":"Passage Creek","difficulty":"IItoIII","states":["USA-VRG"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"16","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Put In - Elizabeth Furnace","id":"109784","location":{"latitude":"38.9291","longitude":"-78.329498"},"name":"Put In - Elizabeth Furnace","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"115036","location":{"latitude":"38.9589","longitude":"-78.266197"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"6","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101249975","title":null,"post_date":"2013-01-31 05:00:00+00","detail":"Ditto on the parking issue ANYWHERE along Route 55. A local cop and a tow truck company are in cahoots. The tow truck driver says he will tow any vehicle he can see from route 55 parked legally or not, even on that dirt road on river right. The Strasbourg police know the company well and not in a favorable way. By the way, the Strasbourg City police are very friendly, professional, and helpful should you get towed. I plan to take out along the Shenandoah instead. The tow truck driver said he has even towed from the fish hatchery parking area.","user":{"name":"Charles Duffy"}},{"id":"101248178","title":null,"post_date":"2011-05-25 04:00:00+00","detail":"Several groups have recently reported having their cars towed when parked along Rt 55 at the take out and nearby gravel road. They advised taking out upstream at the fish hatchery or downstream where it joins the Shenandoah.","user":{"name":"John Sunda"}}],"photos":[{"id":"8794","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7553","description":"This is the first 'real' drop.  At higher levels it forms a halfway decent surf wave with good eddy service.","caption":"First rapid","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8794","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8794"}},{"id":"8793","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7553","description":"This is the putin for the lower 'rapids' section.  An alternate putin/parking lot is further downstream just ahead of the first class II drop.","caption":"Putin","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8793","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8793"}},{"id":"8797","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"I believe this is the top of Out Of Sight.  At this level it's just rock dodging mostly.","caption":"Outta Sight!","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8797","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8797"}},{"id":"wwkiOntekGNh4dkH3Ajhm","ext":"jpg","post_id":"wwkiOntekGNh4dkH3Ajhm","description":"Olin Judy running the drop by the road on the left channel after the creek splits, just upstream from the man made wall on river left that supports the road. 375 cfs.","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2022-05-09T23:37:45.000Z","updated_at":"2022-05-10T03:45:24.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/wwkiOntekGNh4dkH3Ajhm","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/wwkiOntekGNh4dkH3Ajhm"}},{"id":"v8WMrNWJwcjx3jybqqtAi","ext":"jpg","post_id":"v8WMrNWJwcjx3jybqqtAi","description":"Isaac Judy running the roadside drop upstream of the man made rock wall supporting the road, 375 cfs.","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"NicholasCarl33","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2022-05-09T23:47:11.000Z","updated_at":"2022-05-10T03:48:37.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/v8WMrNWJwcjx3jybqqtAi","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/v8WMrNWJwcjx3jybqqtAi"}},{"id":"8796","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"Typical drop.","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8796","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8796"}},{"id":"7610","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4511","description":"boogie water below the fish hatchery dam","caption":"ripples below the dam","poi_name":"","subject":"Chrystal and Mike","author":"John Duke","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7610","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7610"}},{"id":"8798","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"Left at the island...","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8798","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8798"}},{"id":"8799","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"This is the last main drop.  It flows over a couple rocks and pushes against the road support wall.  Nothing threatening, just unusual.","caption":"Last drop","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8799","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8799"}},{"id":"8800","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"What creek isn't complete without one of these?  Don't even THINK of boofing the lip, there's no pool at the bottom.  Flat concrete.\r\n\r\n3 ways of dealing with this.  Portage left and bushwhack, portage right along the concrete wall, or take the tiny, bony side creek channel just before the dam on the left.  ","caption":"The dam","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8800","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8800"}},{"id":"8801","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7552","description":"This strainer is on the lower section below the fish hatchery.  We managed to squeeze in on the left side, but at higher water that wouldn't be an option.","caption":"Strainer below hatchery","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8801","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8801"}},{"id":"8795","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7553","description":"Huge tree down across the entire stream.  Unfortunately a mandatory bushwhack portage.","caption":"Strainer!","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Chris B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8795","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8795"}},{"id":"50732","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"175362","description":"One of the best rapids on the creek","caption":"120 cfm","poi_name":"blue hole","subject":"Philip Benner","author":"Thomas Benner","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50732","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50732"}},{"id":"50731","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"175362","description":"","caption":"1800cfm way to high","poi_name":"red hole","subject":"Philip Benner","author":"Tim Navaro","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50731","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50731"}}],"detail":{"id":"1981","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Passage Creek drains a very beautiful and narrow gorge between two spine-like Massannutten Mountains in Virginia. The creek offers a fairly straightforward introduction to running small streams. The rapids are primarily Class II in nature, with two blind rapids that approach Class III.\n\nThe run begins with Class I-II and fastwater as you exit leave the day-use area at Elizabeth Furnace. The first major drop occurs at a right hand bend. Scouting is advisable because of the propensity to pick up strainers. (Of course, strainers may be encountered anywhere along this creek.) The rapid occurs where the creek first comes back near the road, narrowing down into a left to right chute. You can scout from a vegetation and tree choked island on river right. Another major drop occurs at the _Out Of Sight_ rapid as the creek veers away from the road again. This is best run by eddying out first on the right and picking a line through the rocky set of drops.\n\nBelow the gorge section is a dam backing up water for a nearby fish hatchery. The dam may be runnable on the far left. Scout or portage from small river left trail above the dam. Shortly below here, one can take out at the Fish Hatchery Road bridge for a short run that can be easily repeated.\n\nThe rest of the run is Class I-II with some braiding of the stream into small channels around islands. Strainers are almost always lurking in this section in fast current.\n\nEd Evangelidi warns:\n\n\n\"Use caution at Rte. 55, as many of the landowners there are fed up with boaters. If you park on private property there without permission, you might find that your vehicle has been removed to who knows where. The owner downstream river right is convinced that he owns the river.\n\nBy the way, the section from Rte. 55 to the confluence with the N. Fk. Shenandoah is every bit as pleasant as the stretch above Elizabeth Furnace but requires less water to run (-3Â at Rte. 55). Use caution below the fish hatchery, where there is a gravel bar on the right and an elbow bend on the left where the water often goes under, around & through large strainers. Use the gravel bar to scout or portage. Below this, look for a small channel that goes left. This side channel is almost always better than the strainer-prone right side main channel.\n\nRyan Emanual and others, including myself, can also testify:\n\n\"When it's open, Christina's Mexican Cantina in Strasburg is a great place for post-run provisions and refreshment.\"","averageGradient":"40","maxGradient":"60","length":"5.4","correlations":[{"reachID":"1981","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":"IIItoIV","beginLowRunnable":"150","beginMediumRunnable":"220","beginHighRunnable":"1200","endHighRunnable":"1999"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"PASSAGE CREEK NEAR BUCKTON, VA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"01635500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"16","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"3.25","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"38.95816564","longitude":"-78.2666685"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/01635500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/01635500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"16","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cf